java invented by "jammes gosley".
1991 – Named "Oak": The programming language was initially named Oak after an oak tree.
1992 – Star7 Demo: A prototype called Star7 was demonstrated.
1995 – Renamed Java: Oak was renamed Java and officially introduced.
1996 – JDK 1.0: The first official version of Java Development Kit (JDK 1.0) was released.
2. The Problem
Different devices used different chips and operating systems.
C and C++ programs had to be recompiled or rewritten for different platforms.
Software compatibility was a major problem.
Developers wanted a common programming language that could work on different devices.
3. The Idea: Write Once, Run Anywhere
Java was created to solve this problem.
Write the program once.
Compile it into bytecode.
Run the same bytecode on different platforms using the JVM (Java Virtual Machine).
Example:
A Java program can run on:
Windows
macOS
Linux
Main Goal: Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA).
